    "correct_answer": "B",
    "post_content": "Day 1 of 30: Practical SQL & Business Analytics Challenges\nTopic: Anti-Join: Finding Records Without a Match (Beginner)\n\nA streaming platform marketing team noticed their notifications were promoting shows with 0 subscribers. Here is the SQL interview question they ask to fix it.\n\nThe Business Scenario:\nYou have two production tables:\n1. shows (show_id INT, title VARCHAR, release_year INT)\n2. user_subscriptions (user_id INT, show_id INT, subscribed_at TIMESTAMP)\n\nThe goal is to return every show title that currently has zero subscribers.\n\nThe Challenge:\nWhich SQL query safely and correctly returns all shows with no subscriptions, even if user_subscriptions contains NULL values in show_id?\n\nA) SELECT title FROM shows WHERE show_id NOT IN (SELECT show_id FROM user_subscriptions);\nB) SELECT s.title FROM shows s LEFT JOIN user_subscriptions u ON s.show_id = u.show_id WHERE u.show_id IS NULL;\nC) SELECT s.title FROM shows s INNER JOIN user_subscriptions u ON s.show_id != u.show_id;\nD) SELECT title FROM shows WHERE show_id = NULL;\n\nDrop your answer (A, B, C, or D) in the comments along with your reasoning.\nTomorrow at 01:30 PM IST, we post the complete engineering breakdown and SQL solution in the comments.\n\nNIKHILs TECH Studio | India's Practice Gym for the AI Era\nLearn. Practice. Simulate. Grow.\n\n#SQL #DataAnalytics #DataEngineering #BusinessIntelligence #InterviewPrep #TechCareers #NTSCommand",
    "answer_comment": "Day 1 Solution Breakdown: Anti-Join: Finding Records Without a Match\nCorrect Answer: Option B\n\nWhy this is the correct engineering approach:\nThe SQL Solution:\nSELECT s.title\nFROM shows s\nLEFT JOIN user_subscriptions u\n  ON s.show_id = u.show_id\nWHERE u.show_id IS NULL;\n\nCommon Interview Trap:\nOption A fails silently if user_subscriptions has even a single NULL value. Under SQL three-valued logic, (x NOT IN (1, 2, NULL)) evaluates to UNKNOWN for all rows, returning an empty result set.\n\nKey Engineering Takeaway:\nAlways default to LEFT JOIN ... WHERE right_table.key IS NULL or NOT EXISTS for production anti-joins. They are NULL-safe and index-friendly.\n\nDid you get it right? Share your thought process below.\nNIKHILs TECH Studio | India's Practice Gym for the AI Era",
