    "correct_answer": "C",
    "post_content": "Day 2 of 30: Practical SQL & Business Analytics Challenges\nTopic: NULL Handling: The NOT IN Three-Valued Logic Pitfall (Beginner)\n\nWhy did a query with 50,000 active users suddenly return zero rows after one null record slipped into staging? Let us test your SQL logic.\n\nThe Business Scenario:\nAn e-commerce company tracks orders in:\n- customers (customer_id, full_name)\n- orders (order_id, customer_id, amount)\n\nOne order in the table has customer_id IS NULL due to an anonymous guest checkout test.\n\nThe Challenge:\nWhat happens when you run:\nSELECT full_name FROM customers WHERE customer_id NOT IN (SELECT customer_id FROM orders);\n\nA) It returns all customers who have never placed an order.\nB) It raises a runtime syntax error because of the NULL entry.\nC) It returns exactly 0 rows because NOT IN with a NULL subquery evaluates to UNKNOWN.\nD) It automatically ignores NULLs and behaves like NOT EXISTS.\n\nDrop your answer (A, B, C, or D) in the comments along with your reasoning.\nTomorrow at 01:30 PM IST, we post the complete engineering breakdown and SQL solution in the comments.\n\nNIKHILs TECH Studio | India's Practice Gym for the AI Era\nLearn. Practice. Simulate. Grow.\n\n#SQL #DataAnalytics #DataEngineering #BusinessIntelligence #InterviewPrep #TechCareers #NTSCommand",
    "answer_comment": "Day 2 Solution Breakdown: NULL Handling: The NOT IN Three-Valued Logic Pitfall\nCorrect Answer: Option C\n\nWhy this is the correct engineering approach:\nThe SQL Solution:\nSELECT c.full_name\nFROM customers c\nWHERE NOT EXISTS (\n  SELECT 1 FROM orders o\n  WHERE o.customer_id = c.customer_id\n);\n\nCommon Interview Trap:\nBelieving NOT IN filters out NULLs automatically. SQL evaluates 'x NOT IN (1, NULL)' as '(x != 1) AND (x != NULL)'. Since 'x != NULL' is UNKNOWN, the entire condition evaluates to FALSE or UNKNOWN, suppressing all rows.\n\nKey Engineering Takeaway:\nUse NOT EXISTS or an anti-join (LEFT JOIN / IS NULL) whenever subqueries can contain NULL values. It guarantees predictable boolean filtering.\n\nDid you get it right? Share your thought process below.\nNIKHILs TECH Studio | India's Practice Gym for the AI Era",
