    "common_mistake": "Believing NOT IN filters out NULLs automatically. SQL evaluates 'x NOT IN (1, NULL)' as '(x != 1) AND (x != NULL)'. Since 'x != NULL' is UNKNOWN, the entire condition evaluates to FALSE or UNKNOWN, suppressing all rows.",
    "learning_point": "Use NOT EXISTS or an anti-join (LEFT JOIN / IS NULL) whenever subqueries can contain NULL values. It guarantees predictable boolean filtering."
