    "answer_comment": "Day 4 Solution Breakdown: Monthly Retention: Distinct User Counting\nCorrect Answer: Option A\n\nWhy this is the correct engineering approach:\nThe SQL Solution:\nSELECT COUNT(DISTINCT l1.user_id) AS retained_users\nFROM user_logins l1\nINNER JOIN user_logins l2\n  ON l1.user_id = l2.user_id\nWHERE l1.login_date >= '2026-01-01' AND l1.login_date < '2026-02-01'\n  AND l2.login_date >= '2026-02-01' AND l2.login_date < '2026-03-01';\n\nCommon Interview Trap:\nOption B counts users who logged in twice in January as retained, even if they never visited in February.\n\nKey Engineering Takeaway:\nSelf-joining on user_id with separate date range filters is the most robust, index-friendly method for cohort retention analysis.\n\nDid you get it right? Share your thought process below.\nNIKHILs TECH Studio | India's Practice Gym for the AI Era",
