    "post_content": "Day 7 of 30: Practical SQL & Business Analytics Challenges\nTopic: GROUP BY & HAVING: Email Deduplication Cleanliness (Intermediate)\n\nDuring a CRM migration, thousands of contact records have duplicate email addresses with different casing or leading spaces. How do you isolate them?\n\nThe Business Scenario:\nTable contacts:\n(contact_id INT, email VARCHAR, registered_at TIMESTAMP)\n\nYou need to find all normalized email addresses (lowercase, trimmed) that appear on more than one distinct contact_id.\n\nThe Challenge:\nWhich query correctly finds all duplicated normalized emails and the number of accounts tied to each?\n\nA) SELECT LOWER(TRIM(email)) AS clean_email, COUNT(DISTINCT contact_id) AS accounts FROM contacts GROUP BY LOWER(TRIM(email)) HAVING COUNT(DISTINCT contact_id) > 1;\nB) SELECT email, COUNT(*) FROM contacts WHERE email = LOWER(email) GROUP BY email;\nC) SELECT DISTINCT email FROM contacts WHERE COUNT(contact_id) > 1;\nD) SELECT TRIM(email) FROM contacts GROUP BY contact_id HAVING COUNT(email) > 1;\n\nDrop your answer (A, B, C, or D) in the comments along with your reasoning.\nTomorrow at 01:30 PM IST, we post the complete engineering breakdown and SQL solution in the comments.\n\nNIKHILs TECH Studio | India's Practice Gym for the AI Era\nLearn. Practice. Simulate. Grow.\n\n#SQL #DataAnalytics #DataEngineering #BusinessIntelligence #InterviewPrep #TechCareers #NTSCommand",
    "answer_comment": "Day 7 Solution Breakdown: GROUP BY & HAVING: Email Deduplication Cleanliness\nCorrect Answer: Option A\n\nWhy this is the correct engineering approach:\nThe SQL Solution:\nSELECT \n  LOWER(TRIM(email)) AS clean_email,\n  COUNT(DISTINCT contact_id) AS accounts\nFROM contacts\nGROUP BY LOWER(TRIM(email))\nHAVING COUNT(DISTINCT contact_id) > 1\nORDER BY accounts DESC;\n\nCommon Interview Trap:\nGrouping by raw email without applying normalization (LOWER and TRIM), which treats 'John@domain.com' and 'john@domain.com ' as different emails.\n\nKey Engineering Takeaway:\nTransformations in GROUP BY must mirror your business uniqueness rules to catch subtle data hygiene duplicates.\n\nDid you get it right? Share your thought process below.\nNIKHILs TECH Studio | India's Practice Gym for the AI Era",
    "hashtags": "#SQL #DataAnalytics #DataEngineering #BusinessIntelligence #InterviewPrep #TechCareers #NTSCommand",
