    "post_content": "Day 8 of 30: Practical SQL & Business Analytics Challenges\nTopic: Window Functions: Salary Rankings per Department (Intermediate)\n\nThe classic tech interview benchmark: find the top 2 highest paid employees in every department. Which window function should you pick?\n\nThe Business Scenario:\nTable employees:\n(emp_id INT, name VARCHAR, dept_id INT, salary NUMERIC)\n\nIf two employees in the same department tie for 1st place, both should be considered rank 1, and the next highest salary should be ranked 2.\n\nThe Challenge:\nWhich window function guarantees that ties share the same rank without skipping subsequent ranks?\n\nA) ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY dept_id ORDER BY salary DESC)\nB) RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY dept_id ORDER BY salary DESC)\nC) DENSE_RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY dept_id ORDER BY salary DESC)\nD) NTILE(2) OVER (PARTITION BY dept_id ORDER BY salary DESC)\n\nDrop your answer (A, B, C, or D) in the comments along with your reasoning.\nTomorrow at 01:30 PM IST, we post the complete engineering breakdown and SQL solution in the comments.\n\nNIKHILs TECH Studio | India's Practice Gym for the AI Era\nLearn. Practice. Simulate. Grow.\n\n#SQL #DataAnalytics #DataEngineering #BusinessIntelligence #InterviewPrep #TechCareers #NTSCommand",
    "answer_comment": "Day 8 Solution Breakdown: Window Functions: Salary Rankings per Department\nCorrect Answer: Option C\n\nWhy this is the correct engineering approach:\nThe SQL Solution:\nWITH ranked_emp AS (\n  SELECT \n    emp_id, name, dept_id, salary,\n    DENSE_RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY dept_id ORDER BY salary DESC) as rnk\n  FROM employees\n)\nSELECT emp_id, name, dept_id, salary\nFROM ranked_emp\nWHERE rnk <= 2;\n\nCommon Interview Trap:\nUsing RANK(), which produces ranks 1, 1, 3 (skipping 2) when there is a tie, causing the third-highest person to be excluded from a <= 2 filter.\n\nKey Engineering Takeaway:\nDENSE_RANK() assigns consecutive integer ranks without gaps, making it the correct choice for 'top N unique value levels'.\n\nDid you get it right? Share your thought process below.\nNIKHILs TECH Studio | India's Practice Gym for the AI Era",
