    "option_d": "SELECT rider_id FROM rides WHERE LEAD(status) OVER () = 'cancelled';",
    "correct_answer": "A",
    "post_content": "Day 10 of 30: Practical SQL & Business Analytics Challenges\nTopic: LAG & LEAD: Back-to-Back Ride Cancellations (Intermediate)\n\nA ride-hailing app (RideFlow) wants to detect users who cancel two or more rides consecutively within 1 hour. How do you look at the previous ride?\n\nThe Business Scenario:\nTable rides:\n(ride_id INT, rider_id INT, status VARCHAR, request_time TIMESTAMP)\nWhere status in ('completed', 'cancelled', 'driver_cancelled').\n\nThe Challenge:\nWhich query correctly identifies riders whose immediately preceding ride was also 'cancelled' and occurred within 60 minutes?\n\nA) SELECT rider_id, request_time FROM (SELECT rider_id, status, request_time, LAG(status) OVER (PARTITION BY rider_id ORDER BY request_time) as prev_status, LAG(request_time) OVER (PARTITION BY rider_id ORDER BY request_time) as prev_time FROM rides) t WHERE status = 'cancelled' AND prev_status = 'cancelled' AND request_time <= prev_time + INTERVAL '60 minutes';\nB) SELECT rider_id FROM rides WHERE status = 'cancelled' GROUP BY rider_id HAVING COUNT(*) > 1;\nC) SELECT r1.rider_id FROM rides r1 JOIN rides r2 ON r1.rider_id = r2.rider_id WHERE r1.status = 'cancelled';\nD) SELECT rider_id FROM rides WHERE LEAD(status) OVER () = 'cancelled';\n\nDrop your answer (A, B, C, or D) in the comments along with your reasoning.\nTomorrow at 01:30 PM IST, we post the complete engineering breakdown and SQL solution in the comments.\n\nNIKHILs TECH Studio | India's Practice Gym for the AI Era\nLearn. Practice. Simulate. Grow.\n\n#SQL #DataAnalytics #DataEngineering #BusinessIntelligence #InterviewPrep #TechCareers #NTSCommand",
    "answer_comment": "Day 10 Solution Breakdown: LAG & LEAD: Back-to-Back Ride Cancellations\nCorrect Answer: Option A\n\nWhy this is the correct engineering approach:\nThe SQL Solution:\nWITH flagged_rides AS (\n  SELECT \n    ride_id, rider_id, status, request_time,\n    LAG(status) OVER (PARTITION BY rider_id ORDER BY request_time) AS prev_status,\n    LAG(request_time) OVER (PARTITION BY rider_id ORDER BY request_time) AS prev_time\n  FROM rides\n)\nSELECT DISTINCT rider_id\nFROM flagged_rides\nWHERE status = 'cancelled'\n  AND prev_status = 'cancelled'\n  AND request_time <= prev_time + INTERVAL '60 minutes';\n\nCommon Interview Trap:\nOption B ignores event ordering: a rider could have cancelled in January and again in June with 50 completed rides in between.\n\nKey Engineering Takeaway:\nLAG(column, 1) OVER (PARTITION BY entity ORDER BY timestamp) lets you inspect the exact prior event in sequential event logs.\n\nDid you get it right? Share your thought process below.\nNIKHILs TECH Studio | India's Practice Gym for the AI Era",
    "hashtags": "#SQL #DataAnalytics #DataEngineering #BusinessIntelligence #InterviewPrep #TechCareers #NTSCommand",
