    "post_content": "Day 11 of 30: Practical SQL & Business Analytics Challenges\nTopic: First & Latest Records: Second Booking of the Day (Intermediate)\n\nA hotel chain offers a 20% discount exclusively on a customer's second booking on any given calendar day. How do you isolate only that second booking?\n\nThe Business Scenario:\nTable bookings:\n(booking_id INT, user_id INT, booking_time TIMESTAMP, amount NUMERIC)\n\nUsers may create 1, 2, or 5 bookings in a single day. You need to extract exactly the 2nd booking made by each user per calendar date.\n\nThe Challenge:\nWhich query correctly returns the 2nd booking per user per day?\n\nA) WITH numbered AS (SELECT booking_id, user_id, amount, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY user_id, DATE(booking_time) ORDER BY booking_time ASC) as b_num FROM bookings) SELECT * FROM numbered WHERE b_num = 2;\nB) SELECT booking_id, user_id FROM bookings WHERE booking_id = 2 GROUP BY user_id;\nC) SELECT user_id, MIN(booking_time) FROM bookings GROUP BY user_id HAVING COUNT(*) >= 2;\nD) SELECT booking_id, user_id FROM bookings ORDER BY booking_time LIMIT 1 OFFSET 1;\n\nDrop your answer (A, B, C, or D) in the comments along with your reasoning.\nTomorrow at 01:30 PM IST, we post the complete engineering breakdown and SQL solution in the comments.\n\nNIKHILs TECH Studio | India's Practice Gym for the AI Era\nLearn. Practice. Simulate. Grow.\n\n#SQL #DataAnalytics #DataEngineering #BusinessIntelligence #InterviewPrep #TechCareers #NTSCommand",
    "answer_comment": "Day 11 Solution Breakdown: First & Latest Records: Second Booking of the Day\nCorrect Answer: Option A\n\nWhy this is the correct engineering approach:\nThe SQL Solution:\nWITH ranked_bookings AS (\n  SELECT \n    booking_id, user_id, booking_time, amount,\n    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (\n      PARTITION BY user_id, DATE(booking_time) \n      ORDER BY booking_time ASC\n    ) AS booking_num\n  FROM bookings\n)\nSELECT booking_id, user_id, booking_time, amount\nFROM ranked_bookings\nWHERE booking_num = 2;\n\nCommon Interview Trap:\nUsing LIMIT 1 OFFSET 1 in Option D, which gives the 2nd booking across the entire database rather than partitioned per user per day.\n\nKey Engineering Takeaway:\nROW_NUMBER() with a compound PARTITION BY (user_id, DATE(timestamp)) is the standard solution for ordinal event extraction.\n\nDid you get it right? Share your thought process below.\nNIKHILs TECH Studio | India's Practice Gym for the AI Era",
