    "correct_answer": "A",
    "post_content": "Day 12 of 30: Practical SQL & Business Analytics Challenges\nTopic: ROW_NUMBER: Raw Event Stream Deduplication (Intermediate)\n\nNetwork retries caused your mobile telemetry pipeline to receive duplicate click events with identical timestamps. How do you pick just one?\n\nThe Business Scenario:\nTable raw_click_events:\n(event_uuid VARCHAR, session_id VARCHAR, user_id INT, event_time TIMESTAMP, payload JSON, inserted_at TIMESTAMP)\n\nA duplicate is defined as identical (session_id, user_id, event_time). When duplicates occur, retain the record with the earliest inserted_at.\n\nThe Challenge:\nWhich deduplication pattern retains the earliest inserted record while discarding duplicates?\n\nA) WITH ranked AS (SELECT *, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY session_id, user_id, event_time ORDER BY inserted_at ASC) as rn FROM raw_click_events) SELECT * FROM ranked WHERE rn = 1;\nB) SELECT DISTINCT session_id, user_id, event_time FROM raw_click_events;\nC) SELECT * FROM raw_click_events WHERE inserted_at = MIN(inserted_at);\nD) WITH ranked AS (SELECT *, DENSE_RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY session_id, user_id, event_time ORDER BY inserted_at ASC) as rn FROM raw_click_events) SELECT * FROM ranked WHERE rn = 1;\n\nDrop your answer (A, B, C, or D) in the comments along with your reasoning.\nTomorrow at 01:30 PM IST, we post the complete engineering breakdown and SQL solution in the comments.\n\nNIKHILs TECH Studio | India's Practice Gym for the AI Era\nLearn. Practice. Simulate. Grow.\n\n#SQL #DataAnalytics #DataEngineering #BusinessIntelligence #InterviewPrep #TechCareers #NTSCommand",
    "answer_comment": "Day 12 Solution Breakdown: ROW_NUMBER: Raw Event Stream Deduplication\nCorrect Answer: Option A\n\nWhy this is the correct engineering approach:\nThe SQL Solution:\nWITH deduped_events AS (\n  SELECT \n    *,\n    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (\n      PARTITION BY session_id, user_id, event_time \n      ORDER BY inserted_at ASC, event_uuid ASC\n    ) AS row_num\n  FROM raw_click_events\n)\nSELECT event_uuid, session_id, user_id, event_time, payload\nFROM deduped_events\nWHERE row_num = 1;\n\nCommon Interview Trap:\nUsing DENSE_RANK() in Option D. If two duplicate rows share the exact same inserted_at timestamp, both get rank 1 and duplicates remain.\n\nKey Engineering Takeaway:\nROW_NUMBER() strictly assigns unique incremental integers (1, 2, 3...) per partition, guaranteeing exactly one winner row.\n\nDid you get it right? Share your thought process below.\nNIKHILs TECH Studio | India's Practice Gym for the AI Era",
