    "option_d": "SELECT u.country, p.category_id, SUM(oi.quantity) * SUM(oi.unit_price) FROM orders o JOIN users u ON o.user_id = u.user_id GROUP BY u.country, p.category_id;",
    "correct_answer": "A",
    "post_content": "Day 13 of 30: Practical SQL & Business Analytics Challenges\nTopic: Multi-Table Joins: 4-Table Enterprise Revenue Query (Intermediate)\n\nCan you write an accurate enterprise revenue query joining users, orders, line items, and product categories without inflating totals?\n\nThe Business Scenario:\nTables:\n- users (user_id, country)\n- orders (order_id, user_id, order_date, status)\n- order_items (item_id, order_id, product_id, quantity, unit_price)\n- products (product_id, category_id)\n\nYou need total revenue for completed orders by country and category_id for 2026.\n\nThe Challenge:\nWhich query correctly computes total revenue without multiplying quantities across joins?\n\nA) SELECT u.country, p.category_id, SUM(oi.quantity * oi.unit_price) AS total_revenue FROM orders o JOIN users u ON o.user_id = u.user_id JOIN order_items oi ON o.order_id = oi.order_id JOIN products p ON oi.product_id = p.product_id WHERE o.status = 'completed' AND o.order_date >= '2026-01-01' GROUP BY u.country, p.category_id;\nB) SELECT u.country, p.category_id, SUM(o.total_amount) FROM orders o JOIN users u ON o.user_id = u.user_id JOIN order_items oi ON o.order_id = oi.order_id JOIN products p ON oi.product_id = p.product_id GROUP BY u.country, p.category_id;\nC) SELECT country, category_id, COUNT(*) * AVG(unit_price) FROM order_items GROUP BY country, category_id;\nD) SELECT u.country, p.category_id, SUM(oi.quantity) * SUM(oi.unit_price) FROM orders o JOIN users u ON o.user_id = u.user_id GROUP BY u.country, p.category_id;\n\nDrop your answer (A, B, C, or D) in the comments along with your reasoning.\nTomorrow at 01:30 PM IST, we post the complete engineering breakdown and SQL solution in the comments.\n\nNIKHILs TECH Studio | India's Practice Gym for the AI Era\nLearn. Practice. Simulate. Grow.\n\n#SQL #DataAnalytics #DataEngineering #BusinessIntelligence #InterviewPrep #TechCareers #NTSCommand",
    "answer_comment": "Day 13 Solution Breakdown: Multi-Table Joins: 4-Table Enterprise Revenue Query\nCorrect Answer: Option A\n\nWhy this is the correct engineering approach:\nThe SQL Solution:\nSELECT \n  u.country,\n  p.category_id,\n  SUM(oi.quantity * oi.unit_price) AS total_revenue\nFROM orders o\nINNER JOIN users u \n  ON o.user_id = u.user_id\nINNER JOIN order_items oi \n  ON o.order_id = oi.order_id\nINNER JOIN products p \n  ON oi.product_id = p.product_id\nWHERE o.status = 'completed'\n  AND o.order_date >= '2026-01-01'\nGROUP BY u.country, p.category_id;\n\nCommon Interview Trap:\nOption B sums o.total_amount after joining order_items. Since an order has multiple items, o.total_amount is summed once per item, drastically overstating revenue.\n\nKey Engineering Takeaway:\nCalculate line-level metrics (quantity * unit_price) directly at the leaf grain rather than summing parent headers across 1-to-many relationships.\n\nDid you get it right? Share your thought process below.\nNIKHILs TECH Studio | India's Practice Gym for the AI Era",
