    "option_d": "SELECT campaign_id, SUM(amount) FROM purchases JOIN ad_clicks USING (user_id) GROUP BY campaign_id;",
    "correct_answer": "A",
    "post_content": "Day 15 of 30: Practical SQL & Business Analytics Challenges\nTopic: Campaign Attribution: Last-Touch Conversion Tracking (Intermediate)\n\nA buyer clicked 4 different ads over 2 weeks before finally making a purchase. How do you credit 100% of the sale to the last-clicked campaign?\n\nThe Business Scenario:\nTables:\n1. ad_clicks (click_id, user_id, campaign_id, click_time)\n2. purchases (purchase_id, user_id, amount, purchase_time)\n\nRules: The attributing click must occur before the purchase_time, and be the latest click prior to that purchase.\n\nThe Challenge:\nWhich SQL construct correctly extracts the campaign_id of the final click before each purchase?\n\nA) WITH ranked_clicks AS (SELECT p.purchase_id, c.campaign_id, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY p.purchase_id ORDER BY c.click_time DESC) as rn FROM purchases p JOIN ad_clicks c ON p.user_id = c.user_id AND c.click_time <= p.purchase_time) SELECT purchase_id, campaign_id FROM ranked_clicks WHERE rn = 1;\nB) SELECT p.purchase_id, c.campaign_id FROM purchases p JOIN ad_clicks c ON p.user_id = c.user_id WHERE c.click_time = MAX(c.click_time);\nC) SELECT p.purchase_id, MAX(c.campaign_id) FROM purchases p JOIN ad_clicks c ON p.user_id = c.user_id GROUP BY p.purchase_id;\nD) SELECT campaign_id, SUM(amount) FROM purchases JOIN ad_clicks USING (user_id) GROUP BY campaign_id;\n\nDrop your answer (A, B, C, or D) in the comments along with your reasoning.\nTomorrow at 01:30 PM IST, we post the complete engineering breakdown and SQL solution in the comments.\n\nNIKHILs TECH Studio | India's Practice Gym for the AI Era\nLearn. Practice. Simulate. Grow.\n\n#SQL #DataAnalytics #DataEngineering #BusinessIntelligence #InterviewPrep #TechCareers #NTSCommand",
    "answer_comment": "Day 15 Solution Breakdown: Campaign Attribution: Last-Touch Conversion Tracking\nCorrect Answer: Option A\n\nWhy this is the correct engineering approach:\nThe SQL Solution:\nWITH eligible_clicks AS (\n  SELECT \n    p.purchase_id,\n    c.campaign_id,\n    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (\n      PARTITION BY p.purchase_id \n      ORDER BY c.click_time DESC\n    ) AS rn\n  FROM purchases p\n  INNER JOIN ad_clicks c \n    ON p.user_id = c.user_id \n   AND c.click_time <= p.purchase_time\n)\nSELECT purchase_id, campaign_id\nFROM eligible_clicks\nWHERE rn = 1;\n\nCommon Interview Trap:\nOption C uses MAX(campaign_id), which picks the highest alphabetical string rather than the latest timestamp.\n\nKey Engineering Takeaway:\nLast-touch attribution requires joining prior events (<= purchase_time) and ranking by timestamp DESC with ROW_NUMBER() = 1.\n\nDid you get it right? Share your thought process below.\nNIKHILs TECH Studio | India's Practice Gym for the AI Era",
    "hashtags": "#SQL #DataAnalytics #DataEngineering #BusinessIntelligence #InterviewPrep #TechCareers #NTSCommand",
    "quality_score": 93,
    "sql_solution": "WITH eligible_clicks AS (\n  SELECT \n    p.purchase_id,\n    c.campaign_id,\n    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (\n      PARTITION BY p.purchase_id \n      ORDER BY c.click_time DESC\n    ) AS rn\n  FROM purchases p\n  INNER JOIN ad_clicks c \n    ON p.user_id = c.user_id \n   AND c.click_time <= p.purchase_time\n)\nSELECT purchase_id, campaign_id\nFROM eligible_clicks\nWHERE rn = 1;",
    "common_mistake": "Option C uses MAX(campaign_id), which picks the highest alphabetical string rather than the latest timestamp.",
    "learning_point": "Last-touch attribution requires joining prior events (<= purchase_time) and ranking by timestamp DESC with ROW_NUMBER() = 1."
