    "correct_answer": "A",
    "post_content": "Day 20 of 30: Practical SQL & Business Analytics Challenges\nTopic: Weekly CTR Trend: Detecting Audience Send Fatigue (Intermediate)\n\nYour newsletter open rate dropped from 42% to 18% over 8 weeks. How do you write a rolling week-over-week CTR drop query in SQL?\n\nThe Business Scenario:\nTable email_blasts:\n(blast_id, send_date, emails_sent, clicks)\n\nCompute the weekly Click-Through Rate (SUM(clicks)/SUM(emails_sent)) and the difference from the previous week using window functions.\n\nThe Challenge:\nWhich query correctly calculates the week-over-week change in CTR?\n\nA) WITH weekly AS (SELECT DATE_TRUNC('week', send_date) as wk, 100.0 * SUM(clicks) / SUM(emails_sent) as ctr FROM email_blasts GROUP BY 1) SELECT wk, ctr, ctr - LAG(ctr) OVER (ORDER BY wk) as wow_change FROM weekly;\nB) SELECT send_date, (clicks/emails_sent) - LAG(clicks/emails_sent) OVER () FROM email_blasts;\nC) SELECT DATE_TRUNC('week', send_date), AVG(clicks) - AVG(emails_sent) FROM email_blasts GROUP BY 1;\nD) SELECT wk, LAG(ctr, -1) OVER () FROM email_blasts;\n\nDrop your answer (A, B, C, or D) in the comments along with your reasoning.\nTomorrow at 01:30 PM IST, we post the complete engineering breakdown and SQL solution in the comments.\n\nNIKHILs TECH Studio | India's Practice Gym for the AI Era\nLearn. Practice. Simulate. Grow.\n\n#SQL #DataAnalytics #DataEngineering #BusinessIntelligence #InterviewPrep #TechCareers #NTSCommand",
    "answer_comment": "Day 20 Solution Breakdown: Weekly CTR Trend: Detecting Audience Send Fatigue\nCorrect Answer: Option A\n\nWhy this is the correct engineering approach:\nThe SQL Solution:\nWITH weekly_summary AS (\n  SELECT \n    DATE_TRUNC('week', send_date) AS blast_week,\n    SUM(clicks) AS total_clicks,\n    SUM(emails_sent) AS total_sent,\n    ROUND(100.0 * SUM(clicks) / NULLIF(SUM(emails_sent), 0), 2) AS weekly_ctr\n  FROM email_blasts\n  GROUP BY DATE_TRUNC('week', send_date)\n)\nSELECT \n  blast_week,\n  weekly_ctr,\n  ROUND(weekly_ctr - LAG(weekly_ctr) OVER (ORDER BY blast_week), 2) AS wow_change\nFROM weekly_summary\nORDER BY blast_week ASC;\n\nCommon Interview Trap:\nOption B computes CTR per blast before aggregating to the week level, meaning a small blast with 10 emails carries equal weight to a blast with 500,000 emails.\n\nKey Engineering Takeaway:\nAggregate volumes first (sum clicks / sum sent) before taking ratios, then apply LAG() over the aggregated timeline.\n\nDid you get it right? Share your thought process below.\nNIKHILs TECH Studio | India's Practice Gym for the AI Era",
    "hashtags": "#SQL #DataAnalytics #DataEngineering #BusinessIntelligence #InterviewPrep #TechCareers #NTSCommand",
    "quality_score": 98,
    "sql_solution": "WITH weekly_summary AS (\n  SELECT \n    DATE_TRUNC('week', send_date) AS blast_week,\n    SUM(clicks) AS total_clicks,\n    SUM(emails_sent) AS total_sent,\n    ROUND(100.0 * SUM(clicks) / NULLIF(SUM(emails_sent), 0), 2) AS weekly_ctr\n  FROM email_blasts\n  GROUP BY DATE_TRUNC('week', send_date)\n)\nSELECT \n  blast_week,\n  weekly_ctr,\n  ROUND(weekly_ctr - LAG(weekly_ctr) OVER (ORDER BY blast_week), 2) AS wow_change\nFROM weekly_summary\nORDER BY blast_week ASC;",
    "common_mistake": "Option B computes CTR per blast before aggregating to the week level, meaning a small blast with 10 emails carries equal weight to a blast with 500,000 emails.",
