    "option_d": "LAG(dau, 1) OVER (PARTITION BY app_id ORDER BY stat_date)",
    "correct_answer": "A",
    "post_content": "Day 24 of 30: Practical SQL & Business Analytics Challenges\nTopic: Momentum Drop-Off: Peak-Then-Decline Lifecycle Analysis (Advanced)\n\nA mobile game went viral on TikTok, peaked at 1M DAU, and crashed 80% two weeks later. How do you identify this lifecycle pattern in SQL?\n\nThe Business Scenario:\nTable daily_active_users:\n(app_id INT, stat_date DATE, dau INT)\n\nIdentify all apps where the current DAU has dropped by more than 50% from its lifetime peak DAU recorded on or before that date.\n\nThe Challenge:\nWhich window function correctly tracks the cumulative historical peak DAU up to the current date?\n\nA) MAX(dau) OVER (PARTITION BY app_id ORDER BY stat_date ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW)\nB) MAX(dau) OVER (PARTITION BY app_id)\nC) FIRST_VALUE(dau) OVER (PARTITION BY app_id ORDER BY stat_date)\nD) LAG(dau, 1) OVER (PARTITION BY app_id ORDER BY stat_date)\n\nDrop your answer (A, B, C, or D) in the comments along with your reasoning.\nTomorrow at 01:30 PM IST, we post the complete engineering breakdown and SQL solution in the comments.\n\nNIKHILs TECH Studio | India's Practice Gym for the AI Era\nLearn. Practice. Simulate. Grow.\n\n#SQL #DataAnalytics #DataEngineering #BusinessIntelligence #InterviewPrep #TechCareers #NTSCommand",
    "answer_comment": "Day 24 Solution Breakdown: Momentum Drop-Off: Peak-Then-Decline Lifecycle Analysis\nCorrect Answer: Option A\n\nWhy this is the correct engineering approach:\nThe SQL Solution:\nWITH dau_trajectory AS (\n  SELECT \n    app_id,\n    stat_date,\n    dau,\n    MAX(dau) OVER (\n      PARTITION BY app_id \n      ORDER BY stat_date \n      ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW\n    ) AS peak_dau_to_date\n  FROM daily_active_users\n)\nSELECT \n  app_id, stat_date, dau, peak_dau_to_date,\n  ROUND(100.0 * (peak_dau_to_date - dau) / peak_dau_to_date, 1) AS pct_drop_from_peak\nFROM dau_trajectory\nWHERE dau < 0.5 * peak_dau_to_date;\n\nCommon Interview Trap:\nOption B calculates the overall lifetime maximum across all dates. If the peak occurs in December, filtering early January rows against December's peak creates look-ahead data leakage.\n\nKey Engineering Takeaway:\nCumulative window frames (ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW) track running maximums without look-ahead bias.\n\nDid you get it right? Share your thought process below.\nNIKHILs TECH Studio | India's Practice Gym for the AI Era",
    "hashtags": "#SQL #DataAnalytics #DataEngineering #BusinessIntelligence #InterviewPrep #TechCareers #NTSCommand",
    "quality_score": 95,
    "sql_solution": "WITH dau_trajectory AS (\n  SELECT \n    app_id,\n    stat_date,\n    dau,\n    MAX(dau) OVER (\n      PARTITION BY app_id \n      ORDER BY stat_date \n      ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW\n    ) AS peak_dau_to_date\n  FROM daily_active_users\n)\nSELECT \n  app_id, stat_date, dau, peak_dau_to_date,\n  ROUND(100.0 * (peak_dau_to_date - dau) / peak_dau_to_date, 1) AS pct_drop_from_peak\nFROM dau_trajectory\nWHERE dau < 0.5 * peak_dau_to_date;",
    "common_mistake": "Option B calculates the overall lifetime maximum across all dates. If the peak occurs in December, filtering early January rows against December's peak creates look-ahead data leakage.",
    "learning_point": "Cumulative window frames (ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW) track running maximums without look-ahead bias."
