    "post_content": "Day 25 of 30: Practical SQL & Business Analytics Challenges\nTopic: Aggregate of Aggregates: Best Student Exam Average (Advanced)\n\nWhat is the average of each student's highest 3 exam scores across all semesters? Nested aggregation trips up even experienced engineers.\n\nThe Business Scenario:\nTable exam_scores:\n(student_id INT, subject VARCHAR, exam_date DATE, score NUMERIC)\n\nFor each student, take only their top 3 scores across all subjects, compute their individual average, and then find the overall cohort mean.\n\nThe Challenge:\nWhich query structure correctly computes this two-tier aggregate without illegal nesting like AVG(AVG(score))?\n\nA) WITH ranked AS (SELECT student_id, score, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY student_id ORDER BY score DESC) as rn FROM exam_scores), student_top3 AS (SELECT student_id, AVG(score) as student_avg FROM ranked WHERE rn <= 3 GROUP BY student_id) SELECT ROUND(AVG(student_avg), 2) as cohort_top3_mean FROM student_top3;\nB) SELECT AVG(score) FROM exam_scores GROUP BY student_id HAVING ROW_NUMBER() <= 3;\nC) SELECT student_id, AVG(MAX(score)) FROM exam_scores GROUP BY student_id;\nD) SELECT AVG(score) OVER (PARTITION BY student_id ORDER BY score DESC LIMIT 3) FROM exam_scores;\n\nDrop your answer (A, B, C, or D) in the comments along with your reasoning.\nTomorrow at 01:30 PM IST, we post the complete engineering breakdown and SQL solution in the comments.\n\nNIKHILs TECH Studio | India's Practice Gym for the AI Era\nLearn. Practice. Simulate. Grow.\n\n#SQL #DataAnalytics #DataEngineering #BusinessIntelligence #InterviewPrep #TechCareers #NTSCommand",
    "answer_comment": "Day 25 Solution Breakdown: Aggregate of Aggregates: Best Student Exam Average\nCorrect Answer: Option A\n\nWhy this is the correct engineering approach:\nThe SQL Solution:\nWITH ranked_scores AS (\n  SELECT \n    student_id, score,\n    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (\n      PARTITION BY student_id \n      ORDER BY score DESC\n    ) AS rn\n  FROM exam_scores\n),\nstudent_averages AS (\n  SELECT \n    student_id,\n    AVG(score) AS top3_avg\n  FROM ranked_scores\n  WHERE rn <= 3\n  GROUP BY student_id\n)\nSELECT \n  ROUND(AVG(top3_avg), 2) AS cohort_top3_mean\nFROM student_averages;\n\nCommon Interview Trap:\nAttempting to nest aggregates directly (e.g., AVG(MAX(score))), which violates SQL grammar standards.\n\nKey Engineering Takeaway:\nUse multi-stage CTE pipelines: first rank items, next aggregate per entity, then aggregate across the entire population.\n\nDid you get it right? Share your thought process below.\nNIKHILs TECH Studio | India's Practice Gym for the AI Era",
