    "topic": "P95 Ingestion Lag: Pipeline Health Monitoring",
    "difficulty": "Advanced",
    "hook": "Average pipeline latency is 200ms, but customers are complaining about 10-second delays. Why you must measure P95 latency in SQL.",
    "scenario": "Table ingestion_logs:\n(batch_id VARCHAR, source_name VARCHAR, created_at TIMESTAMP, ingested_at TIMESTAMP)\n\nLatency = EXTRACT(EPOCH FROM (ingested_at - created_at)). You need the 95th percentile latency per source_name for SLA auditing.",
    "question": "Which standard SQL function computes the continuous 95th percentile metric?",
    "option_a": "SELECT source_name, PERCENTILE_CONT(0.95) WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY EXTRACT(EPOCH FROM (ingested_at - created_at))) AS p95_latency_sec FROM ingestion_logs GROUP BY source_name;",
    "option_b": "SELECT source_name, AVG(ingested_at - created_at) * 0.95 FROM ingestion_logs GROUP BY source_name;",
    "option_c": "SELECT source_name, NTILE(95) OVER (ORDER BY ingested_at) FROM ingestion_logs;",
    "option_d": "SELECT source_name, MAX(ingested_at - created_at) * 0.95 FROM ingestion_logs GROUP BY source_name;",
    "correct_answer": "A",
    "post_content": "Day 26 of 30: Practical SQL & Business Analytics Challenges\nTopic: P95 Ingestion Lag: Pipeline Health Monitoring (Advanced)\n\nAverage pipeline latency is 200ms, but customers are complaining about 10-second delays. Why you must measure P95 latency in SQL.\n\nThe Business Scenario:\nTable ingestion_logs:\n(batch_id VARCHAR, source_name VARCHAR, created_at TIMESTAMP, ingested_at TIMESTAMP)\n\nLatency = EXTRACT(EPOCH FROM (ingested_at - created_at)). You need the 95th percentile latency per source_name for SLA auditing.\n\nThe Challenge:\nWhich standard SQL function computes the continuous 95th percentile metric?\n\nA) SELECT source_name, PERCENTILE_CONT(0.95) WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY EXTRACT(EPOCH FROM (ingested_at - created_at))) AS p95_latency_sec FROM ingestion_logs GROUP BY source_name;\nB) SELECT source_name, AVG(ingested_at - created_at) * 0.95 FROM ingestion_logs GROUP BY source_name;\nC) SELECT source_name, NTILE(95) OVER (ORDER BY ingested_at) FROM ingestion_logs;\nD) SELECT source_name, MAX(ingested_at - created_at) * 0.95 FROM ingestion_logs GROUP BY source_name;\n\nDrop your answer (A, B, C, or D) in the comments along with your reasoning.\nTomorrow at 01:30 PM IST, we post the complete engineering breakdown and SQL solution in the comments.\n\nNIKHILs TECH Studio | India's Practice Gym for the AI Era\nLearn. Practice. Simulate. Grow.\n\n#SQL #DataAnalytics #DataEngineering #BusinessIntelligence #InterviewPrep #TechCareers #NTSCommand",
    "answer_comment": "Day 26 Solution Breakdown: P95 Ingestion Lag: Pipeline Health Monitoring\nCorrect Answer: Option A\n\nWhy this is the correct engineering approach:\nThe SQL Solution:\nSELECT \n  source_name,\n  COUNT(*) AS total_batches,\n  ROUND(\n    PERCENTILE_CONT(0.95) WITHIN GROUP (\n      ORDER BY EXTRACT(EPOCH FROM (ingested_at - created_at))\n    )::numeric, \n    2\n  ) AS p95_latency_seconds\nFROM ingestion_logs\nGROUP BY source_name;\n\nCommon Interview Trap:\nOption B computes 95% of the average latency, which is mathematically meaningless and hides extreme tail-latency spikes.\n\nKey Engineering Takeaway:\nPERCENTILE_CONT(percentile) WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY column) computes accurate statistical percentiles for production SLA reporting.\n\nDid you get it right? Share your thought process below.\nNIKHILs TECH Studio | India's Practice Gym for the AI Era",
