    "option_d": "SELECT yr, rider_id FROM (SELECT EXTRACT(YEAR FROM trip_time) as yr, rider_id FROM completed_trips) GROUP BY yr HAVING MAX(trip_id);",
    "correct_answer": "A",
    "post_content": "Day 28 of 30: Practical SQL & Business Analytics Challenges\nTopic: Busiest Rider Per Year Over a 5-Year Window (Advanced)\n\nRideFlow wants to reward their top rider for each calendar year from 2022 to 2026. How do you find the #1 rider in each year in a single query?\n\nThe Business Scenario:\nTable completed_trips:\n(trip_id VARCHAR, rider_id INT, trip_time TIMESTAMP, fare NUMERIC)\n\nFor each year (2022-2026), find the rider_id who completed the highest number of trips in that calendar year.\n\nThe Challenge:\nWhich query correctly extracts the top rider per calendar year?\n\nA) WITH yearly_counts AS (SELECT EXTRACT(YEAR FROM trip_time) as yr, rider_id, COUNT(*) as trip_count, DENSE_RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY EXTRACT(YEAR FROM trip_time) ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC) as rnk FROM completed_trips GROUP BY 1, 2) SELECT yr, rider_id, trip_count FROM yearly_counts WHERE rnk = 1 ORDER BY yr ASC;\nB) SELECT EXTRACT(YEAR FROM trip_time), rider_id, MAX(COUNT(*)) FROM completed_trips GROUP BY 1, 2;\nC) SELECT rider_id, COUNT(*) FROM completed_trips GROUP BY rider_id ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC LIMIT 5;\nD) SELECT yr, rider_id FROM (SELECT EXTRACT(YEAR FROM trip_time) as yr, rider_id FROM completed_trips) GROUP BY yr HAVING MAX(trip_id);\n\nDrop your answer (A, B, C, or D) in the comments along with your reasoning.\nTomorrow at 01:30 PM IST, we post the complete engineering breakdown and SQL solution in the comments.\n\nNIKHILs TECH Studio | India's Practice Gym for the AI Era\nLearn. Practice. Simulate. Grow.\n\n#SQL #DataAnalytics #DataEngineering #BusinessIntelligence #InterviewPrep #TechCareers #NTSCommand",
    "answer_comment": "Day 28 Solution Breakdown: Busiest Rider Per Year Over a 5-Year Window\nCorrect Answer: Option A\n\nWhy this is the correct engineering approach:\nThe SQL Solution:\nWITH rider_annual_totals AS (\n  SELECT \n    EXTRACT(YEAR FROM trip_time) AS trip_year,\n    rider_id,\n    COUNT(*) AS total_trips,\n    DENSE_RANK() OVER (\n      PARTITION BY EXTRACT(YEAR FROM trip_time) \n      ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC\n    ) AS yearly_rank\n  FROM completed_trips\n  WHERE trip_time >= '2022-01-01' AND trip_time < '2027-01-01'\n  GROUP BY EXTRACT(YEAR FROM trip_time), rider_id\n)\nSELECT trip_year, rider_id, total_trips\nFROM rider_annual_totals\nWHERE yearly_rank = 1\nORDER BY trip_year ASC;\n\nCommon Interview Trap:\nOption B uses MAX(COUNT(*)), which is invalid SQL syntax because aggregate functions cannot be directly nested.\n\nKey Engineering Takeaway:\nApply window functions (DENSE_RANK) directly on top of aggregate expressions: DENSE_RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY year ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC).\n\nDid you get it right? Share your thought process below.\nNIKHILs TECH Studio | India's Practice Gym for the AI Era",
