    "topic": "Fraud Detection: Invalid Voucher Redemptions",
    "difficulty": "Advanced",
    "hook": "A referral voucher loophole allowed a bot ring to redeem single-use discount codes 40 times in 3 seconds. Here is the fraud detection query.",
    "scenario": "Tables:\n1. vouchers (voucher_code VARCHAR, max_redemptions INT, expires_at TIMESTAMP)\n2. voucher_redemptions (redemption_id INT, voucher_code VARCHAR, user_id INT, redeemed_at TIMESTAMP)\n\nIdentify all voucher codes where actual redemptions exceeded max_redemptions or where redemptions occurred after expires_at.",
    "question": "Which query flags all offending voucher codes along with the violation reason?",
    "option_a": "SELECT v.voucher_code, COUNT(r.redemption_id) as actual_uses, v.max_redemptions, SUM(CASE WHEN r.redeemed_at > v.expires_at THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) as expired_uses FROM vouchers v JOIN voucher_redemptions r ON v.voucher_code = r.voucher_code GROUP BY v.voucher_code, v.max_redemptions HAVING COUNT(r.redemption_id) > v.max_redemptions OR SUM(CASE WHEN r.redeemed_at > v.expires_at THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) > 0;",
    "option_b": "SELECT voucher_code FROM vouchers WHERE max_redemptions < 1;",
    "option_c": "SELECT r.voucher_code FROM voucher_redemptions r JOIN vouchers v ON r.voucher_code = v.voucher_code WHERE r.redeemed_at > v.expires_at;",
    "option_d": "SELECT voucher_code, COUNT(*) FROM voucher_redemptions GROUP BY voucher_code WHERE COUNT(*) > 1;",
    "correct_answer": "A",
    "post_content": "Day 29 of 30: Practical SQL & Business Analytics Challenges\nTopic: Fraud Detection: Invalid Voucher Redemptions (Advanced)\n\nA referral voucher loophole allowed a bot ring to redeem single-use discount codes 40 times in 3 seconds. Here is the fraud detection query.\n\nThe Business Scenario:\nTables:\n1. vouchers (voucher_code VARCHAR, max_redemptions INT, expires_at TIMESTAMP)\n2. voucher_redemptions (redemption_id INT, voucher_code VARCHAR, user_id INT, redeemed_at TIMESTAMP)\n\nIdentify all voucher codes where actual redemptions exceeded max_redemptions or where redemptions occurred after expires_at.\n\nThe Challenge:\nWhich query flags all offending voucher codes along with the violation reason?\n\nA) SELECT v.voucher_code, COUNT(r.redemption_id) as actual_uses, v.max_redemptions, SUM(CASE WHEN r.redeemed_at > v.expires_at THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) as expired_uses FROM vouchers v JOIN voucher_redemptions r ON v.voucher_code = r.voucher_code GROUP BY v.voucher_code, v.max_redemptions HAVING COUNT(r.redemption_id) > v.max_redemptions OR SUM(CASE WHEN r.redeemed_at > v.expires_at THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) > 0;\nB) SELECT voucher_code FROM vouchers WHERE max_redemptions < 1;\nC) SELECT r.voucher_code FROM voucher_redemptions r JOIN vouchers v ON r.voucher_code = v.voucher_code WHERE r.redeemed_at > v.expires_at;\nD) SELECT voucher_code, COUNT(*) FROM voucher_redemptions GROUP BY voucher_code WHERE COUNT(*) > 1;\n\nDrop your answer (A, B, C, or D) in the comments along with your reasoning.\nTomorrow at 01:30 PM IST, we post the complete engineering breakdown and SQL solution in the comments.\n\nNIKHILs TECH Studio | India's Practice Gym for the AI Era\nLearn. Practice. Simulate. Grow.\n\n#SQL #DataAnalytics #DataEngineering #BusinessIntelligence #InterviewPrep #TechCareers #NTSCommand",
    "answer_comment": "Day 29 Solution Breakdown: Fraud Detection: Invalid Voucher Redemptions\nCorrect Answer: Option A\n\nWhy this is the correct engineering approach:\nThe SQL Solution:\nSELECT \n  v.voucher_code,\n  v.max_redemptions,\n  COUNT(r.redemption_id) AS total_redemptions,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN r.redeemed_at > v.expires_at THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS expired_redemptions\nFROM vouchers v\nINNER JOIN voucher_redemptions r \n  ON v.voucher_code = r.voucher_code\nGROUP BY v.voucher_code, v.max_redemptions\nHAVING COUNT(r.redemption_id) > v.max_redemptions\n    OR SUM(CASE WHEN r.redeemed_at > v.expires_at THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) > 0;\n\nCommon Interview Trap:\nOption C only catches expired redemptions, missing vouchers that were redeemed within the valid time window but exceeded their usage limit.\n\nKey Engineering Takeaway:\nCombine aggregate thresholds (COUNT > max) and conditional sums in a single HAVING clause to catch multifaceted fraud anomalies.\n\nDid you get it right? Share your thought process below.\nNIKHILs TECH Studio | India's Practice Gym for the AI Era",
