    "correct_answer": "A",
    "post_content": "Day 30 of 30: Practical SQL & Business Analytics Challenges\nTopic: Gaps-and-Islands: Consolidating Continuous Status Periods (Advanced)\n\nThe ultimate senior SQL interview challenge: how to collapse consecutive daily active status records into start_date and end_date ranges.\n\nThe Business Scenario:\nTable user_status_history:\n(user_id INT, status_date DATE, status VARCHAR)\n\nA user may be 'active' for 15 consecutive days, 'paused' for 3 days, and 'active' again. You must collapse contiguous periods into:\n(user_id, status, start_date, end_date).\n\nThe Challenge:\nWhich classic difference-of-row-numbers technique groups continuous date islands?\n\nA) status_date - ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY user_id, status ORDER BY status_date) * INTERVAL '1 day'\nB) LAG(status_date) OVER (ORDER BY status_date)\nC) DENSE_RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY status)\nD) status_date + ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY user_id)\n\nDrop your answer (A, B, C, or D) in the comments along with your reasoning.\nTomorrow at 01:30 PM IST, we post the complete engineering breakdown and SQL solution in the comments.\n\nNIKHILs TECH Studio | India's Practice Gym for the AI Era\nLearn. Practice. Simulate. Grow.\n\n#SQL #DataAnalytics #DataEngineering #BusinessIntelligence #InterviewPrep #TechCareers #NTSCommand",
    "answer_comment": "Day 30 Solution Breakdown: Gaps-and-Islands: Consolidating Continuous Status Periods\nCorrect Answer: Option A\n\nWhy this is the correct engineering approach:\nThe SQL Solution:\nWITH status_groups AS (\n  SELECT \n    user_id,\n    status_date,\n    status,\n    -- Subtracting sequence number from date creates a constant group identifier for consecutive days\n    status_date - (ROW_NUMBER() OVER (\n      PARTITION BY user_id, status \n      ORDER BY status_date\n    ) * INTERVAL '1 day') AS island_id\n  FROM user_status_history\n)\nSELECT \n  user_id,\n  status,\n  MIN(status_date) AS start_date,\n  MAX(status_date) AS end_date,\n  COUNT(*) AS duration_days\nFROM status_groups\nGROUP BY user_id, status, island_id\nORDER BY user_id, start_date ASC;\n\nCommon Interview Trap:\nUsing a simple GROUP BY user_id, status. That merges non-consecutive periods together, destroying the chronological timeline.\n\nKey Engineering Takeaway:\nThe 'Difference of Row Numbers' pattern (date - row_number * 1 day) produces a constant anchor date for consecutive days, elegantly solving the Gaps-and-Islands problem.\n\nDid you get it right? Share your thought process below.\nNIKHILs TECH Studio | India's Practice Gym for the AI Era",
    "hashtags": "#SQL #DataAnalytics #DataEngineering #BusinessIntelligence #InterviewPrep #TechCareers #NTSCommand",
